<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franz von Waldeck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1491">1491</a>–<a href="/years/1553">1553</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Francis of Waldeck</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franz von Waldeck was Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Münster from 1532, and also of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>. When radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> seized the city of Münster in 1534, he besieged it with help from neighbouring princes, and his forces took it in June 1535, ending the Anabaptist kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-leiden">Jan of Leiden</a>. He later leaned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> but failed to make the bishopric Protestant.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-von-waldeck.jpg" alt="Franz von Waldeck" width="221" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_von_Waldeck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
